<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berguedà</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Berguedà is a comarca, or county, in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in Catalonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, covering the upper valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llobregat">Llobregat</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berga">Berga</a> as its capital. Its name is traced to the ancient Bergistani. Mountainous in the north, where it reaches the Pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, it developed textile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining industries in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bergueda.jpg" alt="Berguedà" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bsckr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bergued%C3%A0_a_Catalunya.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Berguedà is an inland comarca in the central region of Catalonia, lying partly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and Pre-Pyrenees and partly in the Catalan Central Depression. Its capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berga">Berga</a>. The northern half, Alt Berguedà, takes in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llobregat">Llobregat</a> valley and the mountains around it, and is separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> by a chain of peaks of about 2,000 metres, among them the Cadí, Moixeró, Puig d'Alp and Puigllançada ranges.</p><p>The more populous southern half, Baix Berguedà, lies along the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and includes towns of medieval origin such as Gironella, Casserres and Puig-reig, together with many industrial colonies built along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llobregat">Llobregat</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>. The north is rainy and cold by Catalan standards, with forests of Scots pine and oak, while the south has a dry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> climate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Berguedà has always been sparsely populated. It had 31,544 inhabitants in 1860, a low of 23,257 in 1887 and 27,217 in 1900. For the next sixty years mining, textiles, forestry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranching and agriculture brought a slow but steady increase, to 39,600 in 1930, 41,938 in 1950 and 47,953 in 1960. A fall in the local economy, especially in mining, then coincided with greater opportunity elsewhere, and the population fell to 44,446 in 1970 and 42,152 in 1981. Industry later concentrated between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berga">Berga</a> and Puig-reig, and agriculture, ranching and forestry proved compatible with tourism, which helped to reduce emigration from the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The comarca had 44,632 inhabitants in 2019. It is known for Pedraforca Mountain, traditionally regarded as a meeting place of witches' covens, for its Romanesque churches, medieval bridges and old town centres, notably those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berga">Berga</a> and Bagà, and for the mountain of Queralt, home to a sanctuary of Our Lady of Queralt, the comarca's patroness.</p><p>Its best-known festival is the Patum, held each summer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berga">Berga</a>, in which figures of mythological beasts and historical characters are carried through the streets. On Christmas Eve, Bagà and Sant Julià de Cerdanyola hold the Fia-faia, a pre-Christian tradition marking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>, in which people carry lit bundles of Cephalaria leucantha from a mountain bonfire to the main square.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berguedà” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bergistani lived in the upper Llobregat valley of central Catalonia, a region still called the <b>Berguedà</b> after them.</blockquote><a href="/state/bergistani">Bergistani</a></li></ul></section>
